* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    yes = !sd_admin_tier(user)
*
*    status()  0  the person's register entry carries the ADMINISTRATOR tier
*              1  the register cannot be opened
*              2  no record for this person
*              3  the record carries some other tier, or none
*
*    ***THIS IS HALF OF A TEST AND THE NAME SAYS WHICH HALF.***  Owner, 31 Aug
*    2026: "an administrator has to be both a windows administrator and an sd
*    administrator" - PROJECT_STATUS.md 5.22.  This answers the SD half only.
*    The Windows half is kernel(K$OS.ADMINISTRATOR, 0), and every caller must
*    write it out beside this call:
*
*      if kernel(K$OS.ADMINISTRATOR, 0) and sd_admin_tier(@logname) then
*
*    ***THE WINDOWS HALF IS DELIBERATELY NOT FOLDED IN HERE, AND THAT IS NOT
*    TASTE.***  K$OS.ADMINISTRATOR asks Windows about the REAL uid of THIS
*    process - it takes no user and cannot be asked about anybody else.  A
*    function that took a "user" argument and then answered partly about the
*    caller would give a confident wrong answer to sd_admin_tier('someone.else')
*    and nothing would show.  So the argument means exactly what it says, and
*    the conjunction stays visible at each gate where a reader can see the whole
*    rule.  LOGIN:573 makes the same choice for the same reason and its comment
*    says "Do not simplify this back to one key".
*
*    ***IT IS CALLED EVEN WHEN THE WINDOWS HALF IS FALSE, BECAUSE AND DOES NOT
*    SHORT-CIRCUIT.***  CREATEA:382 records that property, and the defect it
*    cost there went unnoticed from 10 June to 21 Aug 2026.  So every gate that
*    writes the conjunction pays one open, one read and one close per call,
*    including for ordinary users - which is why this refuses cheaply and never
*    aborts: a missing register, an empty name and an absent record are all
*    ordinary @false returns with a status, not errors.  A caller may not
*    assume the argument was even looked at first.
*
*    ONE PLACE THE REGISTER LOGIC LIVES, which is why this is a catalogued
*    function.  There are three gates and they are in two programs: LOGIN's
*    landing case, CPROC's "LOGTO SDSYS" gate, and CPROC's logto.authorised.
*    A predicate copied into two programs is the shape that drifts - a third
*    copy of the administrator verb list cost twenty minutes of a suite run on
*    31 Aug 2026, and that was only a test.
*
*    ***SUSPENDED IS NOT RESOLVED THROUGH ACC$PRIOR.TIER, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE
*    OF TIERGATE AND IS ON PURPOSE.***  !tier_allows keeps a suspended person's
*    RANK so that a grant made while they are suspended is still the right one
*    when it lifts.  This asks whether somebody is an administrator RIGHT NOW,
*    and a suspended one is not.  LOGIN refuses a suspended account at the
*    front door, so the only session this can reach is one that was already
*    running when the suspension landed - which is the case worth denying.
*
*    AN UNKNOWN, EMPTY OR UNREADABLE TIER IS REFUSED, NOT DEFAULTED.  TIERGATE's
*    rule and GRANTA's before it: a missing register must not promote everybody.
*    Note this is the OPPOSITE of NEWVOC's tier lists, where a missing record
*    means the FULL VOC.  Do not carry that convention across.
*
*    ITS OWN FILE VARIABLE, deliberately, and LOGIN's get.acc.tier records why:
*    a caller may have ACCOUNTS open with a select list active on channel 12,
*    and reusing that handle would end the walk part way through "with no error
*    and no sign of it".  CPROC's LOGTO has already closed its own handle by the
*    time logto.authorised runs, so there is nothing to reuse there either.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
$flags trusted

function sd_admin_tier(user)
$catalog !sd_admin_tier

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h

   set.status 0

   sa.who = upcase(trim(user, ' ', 'B'))

   if sa.who = '' then
      set.status 2
      return @false
   end

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to sa.acc.f else
      set.status 1
      return @false
   end

   sa.rec = ''
   read sa.rec from sa.acc.f, sa.who else
      close sa.acc.f
      set.status 2
      return @false
   end

   close sa.acc.f

   if upcase(trim(sa.rec<ACC$TIER>, ' ', 'B')) # 'ADMINISTRATOR' then
      set.status 3
      return @false
   end

   set.status 0
   return @true

* END-CODE
